WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.740
So I want you to imagine something for a second.

00:02.240 --> 00:09.140
Imagine trying to guess, like, the exact weather conditions on the day someone was born just

00:09.140 --> 00:11.280
by watching how they stir their coffee today.

00:11.420 --> 00:13.960
Right, which sounds completely ridiculous.

00:14.120 --> 00:14.440
Exactly.

00:14.620 --> 00:20.180
I mean, if they swirl it clockwise, maybe it was raining, or if they tap the spoon twice,

00:20.300 --> 00:21.080
maybe it was a Tuesday.

00:21.400 --> 00:23.040
It sounds completely absurd.

00:23.340 --> 00:24.820
The connection is totally severed.

00:24.960 --> 00:26.120
Yeah, the time has passed.

00:26.200 --> 00:28.260
Those original variables are just, they're gone.

00:28.400 --> 00:28.600
Right.

00:28.600 --> 00:33.060
But the crazy thing is, that is essentially what astrophysicists are doing with galaxies

00:33.060 --> 00:33.600
right now.

00:33.960 --> 00:39.980
I mean, they're looking at how a massive, mature galaxy spins today, and they are using that

00:39.980 --> 00:47.320
rotation to read the invisible primordial gravitational environment from billions of years ago.

00:47.440 --> 00:50.820
And, well, the truly mind-bending part is that it's actually working.

00:51.100 --> 00:54.160
It's working, and that's our mission for this deep dive.

00:54.160 --> 00:59.400
We are welcoming you, the insanely curious listener, to a really heavy but fascinating

00:59.400 --> 01:00.040
topic today.

01:00.120 --> 01:04.300
Yeah, we are fundamentally rethinking what it means for the universe to, you know, hold

01:04.300 --> 01:04.940
on to a memory.

01:05.260 --> 01:06.740
So let's talk about the source material.

01:07.160 --> 01:12.200
We're grounding this deep dive in a 2026 paper by an independent researcher named Flyxion.

01:12.820 --> 01:16.900
It's called, uh, Primordial Distinctions in Cosmological Memory.

01:16.900 --> 01:17.700
That's right.

01:18.280 --> 01:23.700
And Flyxion builds this really profound theoretical framework on top of a landmark observational

01:23.700 --> 01:25.880
study led by Sheng and their colleagues.

01:26.020 --> 01:28.640
So Sheng's team brought the hard data, right?

01:28.720 --> 01:29.120
Exactly.

01:29.520 --> 01:34.320
They provided the actual astronomical measurements of these galaxies, and Flyxion steps back

01:34.320 --> 01:40.140
to ask, well, what does this discovery actually mean for the nature of information and time

01:40.140 --> 01:40.960
in the cosmos?

01:40.960 --> 01:45.720
And I want to promise you, the listener, right now we're dealing with some heavy astrophysics

01:45.720 --> 01:45.960
here.

01:46.080 --> 01:49.620
We've got tidal torque theory, advanced statistics, spectral mathematics.

01:49.840 --> 01:50.740
It gets deep.

01:50.820 --> 01:51.580
It gets very deep.

01:51.880 --> 01:58.180
But we're going to extract the most incredible aha moments without getting totally bogged down

01:58.180 --> 01:58.640
in the jargon.

01:58.700 --> 01:59.600
We're going to unpack it all.

01:59.700 --> 01:59.960
Definitely.

02:00.200 --> 02:02.340
So let's start with the hard data from Sheng's team.

02:03.040 --> 02:07.580
They looked at present-day galaxies and found this connection to their ancient pre-galactic

02:07.580 --> 02:11.400
seeds, right, the proto-halos.

02:11.640 --> 02:12.560
Yeah, the proto-halos.

02:13.000 --> 02:16.960
And they didn't just find like a vague hint of a connection.

02:17.060 --> 02:17.200
Right.

02:17.240 --> 02:18.400
It was a massive signal.

02:18.540 --> 02:19.440
Far from vague.

02:19.440 --> 02:25.920
They found a statistically recoverable relationship between a galaxy's present-day angular momentum,

02:26.300 --> 02:31.160
you know, its spin, and the primordial tidal field that acted upon its proto-halo.

02:31.220 --> 02:33.820
And the significance level on this was huge, wasn't it?

02:33.820 --> 02:38.960
It was quantified at a 7.0 sigma significance level in their fiducial analysis.

02:39.200 --> 02:39.800
Okay, hold on.

02:39.920 --> 02:45.380
For context, for those who might not follow statistical physics closely, 5 sigma is basically

02:45.380 --> 02:46.840
the gold standard in physics, right?

02:46.880 --> 02:48.100
Yeah, 5 sigma is massive.

02:48.200 --> 02:48.360
Right.

02:48.600 --> 02:51.840
If you hit 5 sigma, you get to hold a press conference and announce you've discovered

02:51.840 --> 02:54.340
a new particle or confirmed a major theory.

02:54.860 --> 03:00.660
The odds of a 5 sigma result being a random fluke are like 1 in 3.5 million.

03:00.660 --> 03:01.220
Exactly.

03:01.460 --> 03:06.400
So hitting 7 sigma, that means the signal is just undeniably mathematically real.

03:06.860 --> 03:07.180
Wow.

03:07.560 --> 03:09.340
Okay, but I have to play devil's advocate here.

03:09.620 --> 03:13.700
If I'm looking at a galaxy, I see this ancient, massive structure.

03:14.440 --> 03:15.940
Why wouldn't it remember its past?

03:16.000 --> 03:17.120
I mean, we remember ours.

03:17.260 --> 03:18.560
A fossil remembers a dinosaur.

03:19.020 --> 03:21.480
Why is this cosmic memory considered so impossible?

03:21.880 --> 03:27.220
Well, because comparing a galaxy to a fossil drastically understates the violence of a galaxy's

03:27.220 --> 03:27.880
life cycle.

03:28.400 --> 03:30.140
I mean, a fossil just sits in the dirt.

03:30.140 --> 03:30.660
True.

03:30.980 --> 03:35.900
A galaxy observed today is absolutely not in the material configuration from which it

03:35.900 --> 03:36.200
began.

03:36.320 --> 03:36.940
It's just not.

03:37.700 --> 03:42.840
To understand why this memory is so astonishing, you have to look at what actually happens to

03:42.840 --> 03:49.900
that initial blob of dark matter and primordial gas over, you know, billions of years.

03:49.940 --> 03:51.200
It gets pretty chaotic, right?

03:51.440 --> 03:52.720
Oh, it's incredibly violent.

03:53.040 --> 03:54.880
First, you have catastrophic shocking.

03:54.880 --> 04:00.360
As the gas collapses under gravity, it heats up to millions of degrees, then it cools, and

04:00.360 --> 04:03.540
then it fragments to form billions of individual stars.

04:03.880 --> 04:06.520
So the continuous cloud of gas is just destroyed.

04:06.860 --> 04:11.100
It's turned into billions of discrete burning points of light.

04:11.220 --> 04:14.420
That alone seems like it would scramble the original structure.

04:14.580 --> 04:15.440
And that is just the beginning.

04:15.740 --> 04:19.020
I mean, the entire structure is then reorganized by massive mergers.

04:19.540 --> 04:21.120
Galaxies do not live in isolation.

04:21.300 --> 04:21.440
Right.

04:21.520 --> 04:22.900
They crash into each other all the time.

04:23.240 --> 04:23.740
Exactly.

04:24.420 --> 04:30.320
When two galaxies collide, the gravitational tidal forces literally rip long trails of

04:30.320 --> 04:32.320
stars out of their original orbits.

04:32.880 --> 04:36.000
The dark matter halos undergo virialization.

04:36.540 --> 04:37.100
Virialization.

04:37.220 --> 04:37.600
What's that?

04:37.600 --> 04:43.880
It basically means they enter a state of chaotic, rapidly fluctuating gravitational equilibrium.

04:44.280 --> 04:46.560
It completely washes out their initial boundaries.

04:46.720 --> 04:47.280
Oh, wow.

04:47.440 --> 04:47.640
Okay.

04:47.820 --> 04:50.020
And on top of that, you have energetic feedback.

04:50.460 --> 04:56.020
You've got supermassive black holes at the center, igniting and blasting immense jets

04:56.020 --> 04:57.200
of radiation outward.

04:57.420 --> 04:57.640
Right.

04:57.740 --> 04:58.480
Like quasars.

04:58.640 --> 04:58.960
Yes.

04:59.380 --> 05:05.380
Plus, thousands of supernovas exploding, literally physically blowing material out of the galaxy.

05:05.380 --> 05:09.400
So it's less like a fossil and more like, I don't know, a sandcastle that gets hit by

05:09.400 --> 05:13.040
a tidal wave and struck by lightning and then trampled by a herd of horses.

05:13.220 --> 05:14.620
That's a pretty good way to picture it.

05:14.680 --> 05:14.860
Yeah.

05:15.040 --> 05:20.620
And yet you were telling me that the final pile of scattered sand somehow still contains

05:20.620 --> 05:24.840
a readable record of the specific breeze that was blowing right as the sandcastle was

05:24.840 --> 05:25.560
first being built.

05:25.780 --> 05:27.140
That is the paradox right there.

05:27.240 --> 05:32.340
I mean, almost zero microscopic configuration from the galaxy's seed remains today.

05:32.680 --> 05:35.140
None of the original puzzle pieces are in the same place.

05:35.140 --> 05:35.580
Exactly.

05:35.800 --> 05:40.940
You cannot trace the material identity of the early density field continuously to today.

05:41.280 --> 05:44.320
The individual particles have been completely reorganized.

05:44.440 --> 05:45.540
But the memory survives.

05:45.840 --> 05:46.160
Yes.

05:46.840 --> 05:51.820
Despite all of that destruction, the current rotation carries information from a primordial

05:51.820 --> 05:56.000
environment that literally no longer exists in any present day form.

05:56.140 --> 05:57.540
That is just wild.

05:57.780 --> 05:58.300
It is.

05:58.480 --> 06:00.920
And this brings us to Flyxion's main thesis.

06:00.920 --> 06:04.260
Flyxion argues this isn't just a quirky anomaly.

06:04.720 --> 06:07.940
It's a case study for a broader rule of the universe.

06:08.140 --> 06:08.420
Which is.

06:08.800 --> 06:13.300
That physical persistence across cosmic time is not about the survival of an object.

06:13.700 --> 06:16.060
It's about the persistence of a distinction.

06:16.560 --> 06:17.060
Okay, wait, wait.

06:17.600 --> 06:21.960
If the original material is completely scrambled, what exactly is doing the remembering?

06:22.360 --> 06:25.940
I mean, if the sandcastle is destroyed, where's the memory stored?

06:25.940 --> 06:27.540
That's the million-dollar question.

06:27.660 --> 06:27.800
Right.

06:27.880 --> 06:33.500
Because we are used to memory requiring a medium, a hard drive, a brain, a piece of paper.

06:34.060 --> 06:37.820
If there's no continuous physical object, how does the distinction persist?

06:38.280 --> 06:43.900
To solve that puzzle, we have to look mechanistically at how a galaxy gets its spin in the first place,

06:43.980 --> 06:47.140
like long before the chaos even begins.

06:47.380 --> 06:48.720
Okay, let's go back to the beginning then.

06:48.720 --> 06:49.040
Right.

06:49.480 --> 06:52.920
This is defined by classical tidal torque theory, or TTT.

06:53.620 --> 06:58.400
This theory explains that the origin of dalactic angular momentum comes from a misalignment

06:58.400 --> 07:01.600
between two specific properties of the early universe.

07:01.700 --> 07:02.500
Okay, two properties.

07:02.580 --> 07:03.120
What's the first one?

07:03.240 --> 07:05.920
First, we have the protohalos inertia tensor.

07:06.240 --> 07:10.780
Okay, let's break down inertia tensor because that sounds really intimidating to a lot of people.

07:11.380 --> 07:17.380
A tensor is essentially just a mathematical matrix that describes how something extends in three-dimensional space.

07:17.380 --> 07:18.840
Yeah, that's a good way to put it.

07:19.080 --> 07:27.140
So we are really just talking about the physical 3D shape and the mass distribution of this initial blob of dark matter and gas.

07:27.500 --> 07:28.400
Yes, exactly.

07:28.820 --> 07:34.020
It describes how stretched, squashed, or lopsided that initial mass distribution is.

07:34.080 --> 07:34.720
Okay, got it.

07:34.760 --> 07:35.880
So that's the shape of the blob.

07:35.940 --> 07:36.860
What's the second element?

07:37.120 --> 07:39.700
The second element is the primordial tidal tensor.

07:40.040 --> 07:42.560
This represents the surrounding gravitational environment.

07:42.980 --> 07:45.120
The environment pushing and pulling on the blob.

07:45.440 --> 07:45.880
Exactly.

07:45.880 --> 07:51.680
So imagine all the other dense patches of matter in the universe surrounding our blob.

07:52.000 --> 07:56.460
They are exerting a gravitational pull, but that pull isn't uniform.

07:56.640 --> 07:59.720
Right, because matter isn't spread out perfectly evenly.

08:00.020 --> 08:00.260
Right.

08:00.540 --> 08:03.360
It's stronger in some directions and weaker in others.

08:03.860 --> 08:07.020
That uneven gravitational field is the title tensor.

08:07.400 --> 08:15.260
Okay, so the twisting force that actually starts to spin is born because these two things don't perfectly line up.

08:15.260 --> 08:15.860
Exactly.

08:16.220 --> 08:23.400
So if the blob was a perfect sphere or if the gravitational pull was perfectly even from all sides, you wouldn't get a spin.

08:23.520 --> 08:24.500
You just get a straight pull.

08:24.640 --> 08:24.880
Correct.

08:25.080 --> 08:27.480
The rotation is a purely relational consequence.

08:28.120 --> 08:34.400
It is the physical manifestation of the misalignment between the object's shape and the environment's gravitational pull.

08:34.400 --> 08:36.060
Okay, the physics makes perfect sense.

08:36.160 --> 08:36.320
Right.

08:36.520 --> 08:41.620
We know how the spin gets started, so why is this considered such an impossible observational problem?

08:41.820 --> 08:45.760
Well, because of what Flyxion identifies as the access problem.

08:45.800 --> 08:46.600
The access problem.

08:46.700 --> 08:46.960
Yeah.

08:48.000 --> 08:52.600
Tidal torque theory is physically correct on paper, but operationally.

08:52.600 --> 08:54.060
It hits a massive wall.

08:54.780 --> 09:02.900
To prove the theory observationally, you need to measure the tidal field and the inertia tensor in the early universe and then compare them to the galaxy today.

09:03.160 --> 09:04.500
But I'm guessing we can't do that.

09:04.580 --> 09:04.940
No.

09:05.200 --> 09:08.460
We can only ever access one of those two ancient variables.

09:08.820 --> 09:09.420
Let me guess.

09:10.420 --> 09:15.560
We can figure out the gravitational tidal field, but we can't figure out the shape of the blob.

09:15.560 --> 09:16.560
You nailed it.

09:17.120 --> 09:21.860
On large cosmic scales, gravity is relatively well-behaved and deterministic.

09:22.480 --> 09:31.400
So, using the large-scale distribution of matter we see today, we can mathematically run the clock backward to reconstruct the ancient primordial tidal field.

09:31.640 --> 09:31.900
Okay.

09:32.000 --> 09:36.660
But we absolutely cannot reconstruct the original shape, the inertia tensor of the proto-halo.

09:36.840 --> 09:37.520
But why not?

09:37.580 --> 09:41.660
I mean, if we can rewind the gravitational field, why can't we just rewind the blob itself?

09:41.660 --> 09:47.740
Because the boundary of that proto-halo wasn't defined by any distinguishing feature present in the initial conditions.

09:48.020 --> 09:49.200
Wait, I'm losing you a bit.

09:49.260 --> 09:49.660
What do you mean?

09:49.960 --> 10:01.020
So, in the very early universe, there were no discrete objects, there were no hard boundaries, there was just a continuous field of matter with very slight ripples in density.

10:01.460 --> 10:03.000
Okay, so just like a soup of matter.

10:03.100 --> 10:04.120
Yeah, exactly.

10:04.800 --> 10:09.880
Our specific proto-halo was just a slightly denser patch in a universe full of dense patches.

10:10.180 --> 10:11.500
It hadn't collapsed yet.

10:11.500 --> 10:12.400
Ah, I see.

10:12.460 --> 10:18.800
Because it hadn't undergone non-linear collapse, its shape was basically an arbitrary mathematical designation.

10:19.180 --> 10:25.760
It wasn't a physical object that we can retroactively trace back from the messy, randomized galaxy we see today.

10:25.860 --> 10:26.160
I see.

10:26.260 --> 10:27.040
Okay, this makes sense.

10:27.300 --> 10:33.040
So, it's like we have the cookie cutter, you know, the gravitational tidal field shaping things from the outside.

10:33.240 --> 10:34.080
I like that analogy.

10:34.380 --> 10:34.700
Right.

10:34.700 --> 10:40.220
But the original blob of dough, the proto-halo's shape, has been baked, eaten, and digested.

10:40.600 --> 10:45.740
We can't see the original shape of the dough, which means we can't prove how the cookie cutter interacted with it.

10:45.740 --> 10:46.220
Exactly.

10:46.220 --> 10:49.900
And if half the equation is invisible, the whole theory seems totally intestinal.

10:50.000 --> 10:50.220
Right.

10:50.480 --> 10:58.180
And this divergence between what the physics requires and what we can actually observe marks a crucial epistemic boundary.

10:58.740 --> 11:02.060
We simply can't observe the physical relation directly.

11:02.060 --> 11:02.960
So, what do we do?

11:03.320 --> 11:07.020
Well, Shang and their team had to find a clever workaround.

11:07.680 --> 11:13.620
They had to abandon the search for the inertia tensor and find what Flyxion calls a reconstructible proxy.

11:13.840 --> 11:15.140
A reconstructible proxy.

11:15.380 --> 11:15.540
Yeah.

11:15.700 --> 11:18.540
So, they needed a mathematical stand-in for the missing cookie dough.

11:18.940 --> 11:19.380
Exactly.

11:19.920 --> 11:23.480
But, how do you substitute something you fundamentally cannot know?

11:23.600 --> 11:25.820
I mean, if we don't know the shape, how do we fake it?

11:25.820 --> 11:26.760
You don't fake it.

11:26.840 --> 11:31.920
You substitute it with a different, fully reconstructible property that behaves similarly.

11:32.160 --> 11:32.620
Okay, how?

11:32.740 --> 11:42.880
Instead of trying to guess the unobservable shape of the proto-halo, Shang's team replaced it with the tidal field itself, but evaluated it at a completely different, smaller spatial resolution.

11:43.120 --> 11:43.360
Okay.

11:43.780 --> 11:50.580
Specifically, they evaluated it at a smoothing scale of approximately 1.8 times the Lagrangian radius of the halo.

11:50.860 --> 11:52.220
Okay, let's slow down and unpack that.

11:52.220 --> 11:53.820
The Lagrangian radius.

11:55.240 --> 12:01.840
In fluid dynamics, Lagrangian perspective means you are following a specific parcel of matter as it moves, right?

12:01.840 --> 12:03.280
Yes, that's exactly right.

12:03.740 --> 12:11.740
So, the Lagrangian radius here is the size of the original region of space that contained all the mass that would eventually collapse into the galaxy.

12:12.060 --> 12:12.380
Yes.

12:12.620 --> 12:17.140
So, they take that radius and they zoom in slightly to 0.8 times that size.

12:17.320 --> 12:17.580
Okay.

12:17.580 --> 12:22.740
They are looking at the gravitational field at the scale of the inner core of the proto-halo.

12:22.940 --> 12:30.460
This smaller scale traces the local unevenness, the anisotropy, at the exact site where the galaxy is going to form.

12:30.540 --> 12:30.980
Oh, wow.

12:31.300 --> 12:32.600
Okay, I think I see where this is going.

12:32.740 --> 12:32.920
Yeah.

12:32.920 --> 12:40.200
This local gravitational unevenness mathematically stands in for the unobservable shape of the mass itself.

12:40.820 --> 12:46.940
Then, they compare that to the gravitational field at a much larger scale, which characterizes the surrounding environment.

12:47.280 --> 12:49.220
This is a wild conceptual leap.

12:49.340 --> 12:49.520
Yeah.

12:49.520 --> 12:51.960
So, they aren't looking at an object in a field anymore.

12:52.120 --> 12:59.500
They took the unobservable object out entirely, and they are just comparing the gravitational field to itself at two different zoom levels.

13:00.220 --> 13:06.060
They are measuring how much the gravitational environment disagrees with itself from the inner scale to the outer scale.

13:06.200 --> 13:09.320
That is the absolute genius of the reconstructible proxy.

13:09.320 --> 13:18.520
They substituted a pairing between an object and a field with a pairing of the same field with itself, using nested Gaussian smoothing scales.

13:18.540 --> 13:20.300
A nested Gaussian smoothing scale.

13:20.320 --> 13:20.500
Yeah.

13:20.780 --> 13:23.580
It is a scale-dependent non-commensurability.

13:23.960 --> 13:24.860
That's a mouthful.

13:25.160 --> 13:26.880
It is, but it works beautifully.

13:27.480 --> 13:34.380
And to actually execute this, to build this proxy, Sheng's team had to mount an observational effort of staggering scale.

13:34.440 --> 13:34.940
I can imagine.

13:34.940 --> 13:41.200
To reconstruct that ancient gravitational cookie cutter, they used something called the Eel Elusi project.

13:41.420 --> 13:42.100
Eel Ezea.

13:42.520 --> 13:45.320
How does a project like that actually rewind time?

13:45.540 --> 13:48.720
Because earlier you said gravity on large scales is deterministic.

13:49.100 --> 13:52.780
But how do they physically process the data to get the primordial map?

13:53.040 --> 13:59.060
They use a technique called Hamiltonian Markov-Chain Monte Carlo procedures, combined with particle mesh dynamics.

13:59.240 --> 14:01.280
Okay, we definitely need to translate that for everyone.

14:01.280 --> 14:06.780
A Markov-Chain Monte Carlo, or MCMC, is a statistical algorithm, right?

14:06.860 --> 14:07.560
It is, yeah.

14:07.620 --> 14:10.300
It's used to sample complex probability distributions.

14:10.860 --> 14:17.360
You essentially take random walks through a landscape of possibilities, and the algorithm pulls you toward the most likely solutions.

14:17.520 --> 14:18.280
Yes, exactly.

14:18.620 --> 14:23.220
So they take the current observed distribution of galaxy groups in the local universe.

14:23.600 --> 14:24.360
That is the endpoint.

14:24.680 --> 14:25.320
The present day.

14:25.320 --> 14:25.740
Right.

14:26.040 --> 14:37.900
Then, the MCMC algorithm creates millions of potential starting points, primordial density fields, and runs them forward through simplified gravitational simulations using particle mesh dynamics.

14:38.080 --> 14:38.300
Okay.

14:38.480 --> 14:44.320
It compares the simulated endpoint to the real universe, adjusts the starting parameters, and runs it again.

14:44.520 --> 14:44.980
Oh, wow.

14:45.160 --> 14:47.660
So it's trial and error on a massive scale.

14:47.660 --> 14:48.220
Huge.

14:48.580 --> 14:57.000
It is a massive iterative optimization process that eventually converges on the most highly probable map of the early universe's density field.

14:57.140 --> 15:05.000
So they use supercomputers to systematically guess and check until they find the exact primordial map that results in the universe we live in today.

15:05.300 --> 15:06.140
Basically, yes.

15:06.400 --> 15:07.060
That's incredible.

15:07.220 --> 15:09.420
And that gives them the nested tidal fields.

15:09.600 --> 15:09.740
Yeah.

15:09.840 --> 15:12.380
But they also need the modern galaxies to compare it to, right?

15:12.380 --> 15:12.900
Exactly.

15:13.360 --> 15:17.740
For the present day kinematics, they use the Manji Integral Field Survey.

15:17.920 --> 15:18.280
Manji.

15:18.400 --> 15:18.540
Yeah.

15:18.640 --> 15:20.260
And this wasn't just a handful of galaxies.

15:21.020 --> 15:29.860
They analyzed 781 central elliptical galaxies and 815 spiral galaxies tracing the rotation of the gas.

15:29.880 --> 15:30.440
That's a lot.

15:30.700 --> 15:35.960
Plus 635 ellipticals and 812 spirals tracing the rotation of the stars.

15:36.280 --> 15:36.780
Wow.

15:36.780 --> 15:45.520
And they restricted all of this to massive dark matter halos over 10 to the 12th solar masses where the signal should be most robust.

15:45.940 --> 15:46.660
10 to the 12th.

15:46.660 --> 15:47.960
That is a massive scale.

15:48.100 --> 15:51.840
So they have thousands of galaxies mapped in three dimensions.

15:52.100 --> 15:52.360
Yes.

15:52.480 --> 15:58.780
So they take the present day spin from Manji and they cross-reference it against the nested scale proxy from Eulucid.

15:59.580 --> 15:59.840
Right.

15:59.940 --> 16:03.260
And that is exactly where they found the 7.0 sigma correlation.

16:03.540 --> 16:05.440
The math completely checked out.

16:05.440 --> 16:05.700
Okay.

16:06.060 --> 16:06.480
But hold on.

16:06.520 --> 16:10.320
If they completely swapped out the variables, aren't they testing a completely different theory now?

16:10.560 --> 16:13.240
I mean, that sounds like a bit of a statistical sleight of hand.

16:13.340 --> 16:13.580
Yeah.

16:13.580 --> 16:21.080
If I replace the cookie dough with the theoretical model of dough and it bakes perfectly, I haven't proven anything about the original dough.

16:21.500 --> 16:23.820
I've just proven my model works with my cookie cutter.

16:24.020 --> 16:28.700
This is a vital distinction, and it's the exact trap Flyxion warns about in the paper.

16:28.700 --> 16:38.980
The physical claim of classical tidal torque theory and the operational claim actually tested by Sheng are related, but they are absolutely not identical.

16:39.300 --> 16:39.400
Okay.

16:39.560 --> 16:41.720
There are three distinct objects in play here.

16:41.880 --> 16:43.260
Walk me through the three objects.

16:43.320 --> 16:43.500
Sure.

16:43.500 --> 16:46.600
Object one is the physical primordial relation.

16:46.980 --> 16:52.260
That's the true misalignment between the actual proto-halo shape and the true tidal field.

16:52.460 --> 16:52.700
Okay.

16:52.880 --> 16:56.820
This is physical reality, but as we discussed, it is forever unobservable.

16:56.960 --> 16:57.180
Right.

16:57.380 --> 16:59.560
Object two is the reconstructable proxy.

17:00.100 --> 17:04.720
This is that nested two-scale field comparison that Sheng's team computed.

17:05.060 --> 17:05.640
The stand-in.

17:05.640 --> 17:06.240
Exactly.

17:07.040 --> 17:11.960
And object three is the present-day observable, the galaxy's actual measured spin.

17:12.540 --> 17:16.640
The 7.0 sigma correlation is strictly between object two and object three.

17:16.840 --> 17:19.400
So they correlated the proxy to the modern galaxy.

17:19.540 --> 17:22.880
They never actually touched the true primordial restriction.

17:23.000 --> 17:23.120
Correct.

17:23.640 --> 17:27.100
The evidence bears on the first object, but it is not identical to it.

17:27.420 --> 17:28.680
So how do we know it's valid?

17:28.680 --> 17:34.300
The entire validity of this approach relies on immense n-body simulations, demonstrating

17:34.300 --> 17:40.300
that the proxy, object two, actually does tightly track the physical relation, object one.

17:40.540 --> 17:40.880
I see.

17:41.400 --> 17:46.440
The simulations confirm that the local unevenness of the gravitational field is an incredibly

17:46.440 --> 17:49.460
reliable predictor of the resulting mass shape.

17:50.240 --> 17:51.800
It is not just a mathematical trick.

17:51.920 --> 17:54.620
It's a rigorously validated stand-in.

17:54.820 --> 17:54.980
Okay.

17:55.060 --> 17:57.120
So the proxy represents a real physical truth.

17:57.120 --> 17:59.340
We've basically solved the access problem.

17:59.940 --> 18:03.540
But this brings us back to what I think is the most mind-bending part of Flyxion's paper.

18:04.120 --> 18:08.080
We established earlier that the modern galaxy itself has been completely scrambled over

18:08.080 --> 18:09.540
billions of years, right?

18:09.640 --> 18:10.500
Utterly scrambled.

18:10.660 --> 18:15.440
So how does this proxy, this ancient relationship, actually survive in the present day?

18:16.260 --> 18:20.240
And Flyxion talks about this in terms of shifting our framework from viewing systems

18:20.240 --> 18:22.800
as states to viewing them as trajectories.

18:22.980 --> 18:24.900
What does that mean in a physical sense?

18:24.900 --> 18:30.680
It is a profound philosophical reframing of how we conceptualize astronomical bodies.

18:30.820 --> 18:34.680
Usually, when we look at a galaxy, we view it as a state.

18:34.800 --> 18:35.520
Like a snapshot.

18:35.900 --> 18:36.460
Exactly.

18:36.680 --> 18:41.520
A specific collection of matter, gas, and stars existing at a specific spatial coordinate

18:41.520 --> 18:42.140
right now.

18:42.320 --> 18:42.520
Right.

18:42.520 --> 18:48.720
But if a galaxy is purely its present state, then a correlation between that state and

18:48.720 --> 18:54.340
a primordial field from billions of years ago looks completely paradoxical, because the

18:54.340 --> 18:58.180
matter currently present has no physical obligation to remember anything.

18:58.500 --> 18:58.720
Right.

18:58.780 --> 19:02.620
Because if I look at a galaxy as a collection of stars now, those specific stars didn't

19:02.620 --> 19:05.320
even exist when the tidal field was pulling on the proto-halo.

19:05.480 --> 19:07.120
The matter has been totally recycled.

19:07.120 --> 19:07.680
Exactly.

19:08.460 --> 19:13.920
But Flyxion argues, we must view a physical system as a trajectory, a continuous path through

19:13.920 --> 19:16.880
a vast space of admissible configurations over time.

19:17.040 --> 19:17.300
Okay.

19:17.500 --> 19:22.600
Certain observable properties, like total angular momentum, are not just functions of the present

19:22.600 --> 19:22.900
moment.

19:23.100 --> 19:26.080
They are functional representations of the entire trajectory.

19:26.660 --> 19:27.700
They are partly historical.

19:28.080 --> 19:28.580
Oh, I see.

19:28.780 --> 19:32.500
So angular momentum isn't just a sticker slapped on the galaxy today.

19:32.660 --> 19:35.620
It's a summary of what the galaxy has been through.

19:35.620 --> 19:36.600
Yes, exactly.

19:37.060 --> 19:40.480
And this introduces Flyxion's definition of a distinction.

19:40.900 --> 19:41.140
Right.

19:41.240 --> 19:42.580
The persistence of a distinction.

19:42.760 --> 19:42.860
Right.

19:43.180 --> 19:48.620
A distinction is a physically instantiated difference between two alternative histories.

19:48.920 --> 19:49.180
Okay.

19:49.300 --> 19:49.880
Give me an example.

19:50.520 --> 19:54.540
Imagine two slightly different primordial gravitational environments.

19:55.120 --> 20:00.780
If those two environments produce descendant galaxies whose spins we can still statistically

20:00.780 --> 20:04.180
tell apart today, then a distinction has survived.

20:04.440 --> 20:04.840
Wow.

20:04.840 --> 20:10.020
Even if the galaxies themselves have completely changed their material form, the difference

20:10.020 --> 20:14.020
between their historical trajectories remains mathematically recoverable.

20:14.300 --> 20:16.640
Flyxion calls this memory without representation.

20:16.900 --> 20:17.100
Right.

20:17.320 --> 20:19.260
Which sounds almost contradictory to me.

20:19.360 --> 20:21.600
How can you have memory without a record?

20:21.700 --> 20:24.120
If I want to remember something, I write it down, I represent it.

20:24.180 --> 20:24.400
Right.

20:24.520 --> 20:25.040
You store it.

20:25.300 --> 20:25.740
Exactly.

20:26.260 --> 20:27.700
The galaxy doesn't have a hard drive.

20:27.780 --> 20:31.520
But if I'm understanding this correctly, it's kind of like a river that carves a canyon.

20:31.680 --> 20:31.880
Okay.

20:31.900 --> 20:32.340
Let's hear it.

20:32.340 --> 20:34.960
The water that did the carving is long gone.

20:35.600 --> 20:36.800
It vanished into the ocean.

20:37.520 --> 20:39.440
There is no water left to represent the river.

20:40.140 --> 20:45.480
But the shape of the canyon, the depth and the curves, it still tells you how fast and in

20:45.480 --> 20:47.060
what direction the river was flowing.

20:47.600 --> 20:48.140
That's good.

20:48.140 --> 20:51.860
That perfectly captures the essence of it.

20:51.860 --> 20:55.800
A galaxy does not store a symbolic record of the tidal tensor.

20:56.220 --> 20:57.640
There is no memory address.

20:57.980 --> 20:58.060
Right.

20:58.140 --> 20:59.180
The memory is structural.

20:59.640 --> 21:03.180
It is a property of the joint statistics of the system across time.

21:03.560 --> 21:06.360
It is the survival of inferential dependence.

21:06.360 --> 21:11.720
The current spin is statistically dependent on the ancient environment, requiring neither

21:11.720 --> 21:14.600
an explicit representation nor a storage device.

21:14.820 --> 21:17.520
But I have to push back on my own canyon analogy here.

21:17.620 --> 21:19.760
Because a canyon just sits there quietly in the desert.

21:20.160 --> 21:22.460
A galaxy is constantly being hit by other galaxies.

21:22.500 --> 21:22.840
It's true.

21:22.920 --> 21:23.560
It's very active.

21:23.560 --> 21:28.060
And if the universe is constantly moving toward higher entropy, getting messier and more

21:28.060 --> 21:35.320
disordered, how does this fragile geometric memory survive billions of years of chaotic

21:35.320 --> 21:35.800
collisions?

21:36.060 --> 21:38.200
That is the ultimate test of the theory.

21:38.580 --> 21:44.260
Flyxion addresses it head on with a concept called Markovian coarse-grained evolution.

21:44.420 --> 21:46.260
Markovian coarse-grained evolution.

21:46.340 --> 21:46.580
Yes.

21:47.080 --> 21:51.000
And to understand it, we apply something called the data processing inequality.

21:51.320 --> 21:53.240
The data processing inequality.

21:53.240 --> 21:54.360
Let's break that down.

21:54.880 --> 22:00.000
In information theory, it essentially says that you cannot create new information by

22:00.000 --> 22:03.480
just processing or manipulating existing data.

22:03.980 --> 22:06.700
Information can only be lost or maintained along a chain.

22:06.980 --> 22:07.900
It can never increase.

22:08.100 --> 22:08.540
Exactly.

22:08.900 --> 22:15.660
If you have a chain of events, say event A causes event B, which causes event C, the information

22:15.660 --> 22:20.040
about event A that survives in event C can only decrease or at best A the same.

22:20.120 --> 22:21.260
It cannot increase.

22:21.360 --> 22:21.780
Makes sense.

22:21.780 --> 22:26.880
So all influence of the primordial distinction on today's galaxy is mediated through every

22:26.880 --> 22:28.100
single intermediate state.

22:28.620 --> 22:30.180
Every merger, every supernova.

22:30.760 --> 22:34.360
Therefore, accessibility to the primordial distinction must intrinsically decay.

22:34.740 --> 22:35.840
So the memory is fading.

22:36.020 --> 22:37.640
The universe is actively erasing it.

22:37.840 --> 22:38.960
It is fading, yes.

22:39.640 --> 22:44.460
The empirical question is not whether the memory decays, but how slowly it decays relative

22:44.460 --> 22:46.160
to the violence of the transformations.

22:46.160 --> 22:48.100
Okay, do we have numbers on that?

22:48.260 --> 22:48.640
We do.

22:49.180 --> 22:54.480
In simplified computer simulations of just dark matter, the correlation between the

22:54.480 --> 22:58.320
primordial field and the final spin is around 0.5 to 0.7.

22:58.440 --> 22:58.920
Pretty strong.

22:59.120 --> 23:03.380
But in the real messy universe, Shang measured a correlation of 0.13.

23:03.640 --> 23:04.260
Oh, wow.

23:04.260 --> 23:08.580
So the signal has degraded significantly, but crucially, it has not reached 0.

23:08.580 --> 23:09.300
But wait.

23:09.860 --> 23:14.880
When two massive galaxies smash together in a major merger, the gravitational violence

23:14.880 --> 23:15.600
is immense.

23:16.120 --> 23:18.380
How does the spin not just completely reset?

23:18.640 --> 23:19.560
It's a great question.

23:19.640 --> 23:23.180
Why doesn't that merger just wipe the slate clean and push the correlation all the way

23:23.180 --> 23:23.520
to 0?

23:23.520 --> 23:26.800
This is where Flyxion introduces a vital mechanical concept.

23:27.300 --> 23:28.720
Repair without restoration.

23:29.120 --> 23:30.320
Repair without restoration.

23:30.540 --> 23:30.680
Yes.

23:31.100 --> 23:35.100
When a galaxy undergoes a violent merger, it does not rebuild its old self.

23:35.500 --> 23:36.660
It doesn't reverse time.

23:37.060 --> 23:39.820
But it also doesn't just shatter into random chaos.

23:40.100 --> 23:41.120
It undergoes repair.

23:41.520 --> 23:46.380
See, when I hear repair, I think of fixing a broken window so it looks exactly like the

23:46.380 --> 23:46.860
old window.

23:46.960 --> 23:47.120
Yeah.

23:47.120 --> 23:48.280
Returning to the original state.

23:48.360 --> 23:48.560
Right.

23:48.660 --> 23:50.340
In our everyday language, that's what it means.

23:50.340 --> 23:54.220
But in phase space dynamics, repair means something specific.

23:54.960 --> 24:00.580
It means the dynamics return the perturbed system to an admissible region of configuration

24:00.580 --> 24:01.340
space.

24:01.480 --> 24:01.660
Okay.

24:01.920 --> 24:05.740
The galaxy relaxes into a new dynamically viable state.

24:06.300 --> 24:10.220
When two galaxies merge, the system undergoes violent relaxation.

24:10.820 --> 24:15.580
It might end up with a completely different mass, different morphological shapes, say, turning

24:15.580 --> 24:20.320
from two spirals into a giant elliptical and completely different internal space.

24:20.580 --> 24:20.980
Kinematics.

24:21.180 --> 24:21.460
Okay.

24:21.720 --> 24:25.720
But during this relaxation process, it retains relational invariance.

24:25.860 --> 24:27.120
It finds a new equilibrium.

24:27.560 --> 24:31.280
And in that new equilibrium, the ancient rotational bias is preserved.

24:31.460 --> 24:31.980
Oh, I get it.

24:32.020 --> 24:35.020
So it's not repairing the window to look exactly like the old window.

24:35.120 --> 24:35.400
No.

24:35.540 --> 24:40.380
It's more like taking a lump of blue clay and a lump of yellow clay, both spinning clockwise.

24:40.780 --> 24:42.560
You smash them together violently.

24:42.720 --> 24:42.860
Right.

24:43.080 --> 24:45.440
You don't get the individual blue and yellow lumps back.

24:45.500 --> 24:47.040
The original configuration is lost.

24:47.040 --> 24:47.400
Mm-hmm.

24:47.500 --> 24:49.460
You get a new, larger green lump.

24:49.820 --> 24:50.340
Exactly.

24:50.580 --> 24:52.820
That is repairing into a new admissible state.

24:53.460 --> 24:56.140
But the new green lump is still spinning clockwise.

24:56.800 --> 25:02.160
The material state is destroyed, but the relational memory, the spin, survives the chaos.

25:02.160 --> 25:04.460
That captures the dynamic perfectly.

25:05.200 --> 25:09.060
Repair preserves difference rather than restoring sameness.

25:09.300 --> 25:09.620
Wow.

25:10.060 --> 25:15.240
The galaxy's overall configuration is free to change entirely, while a small number of

25:15.240 --> 25:18.720
relational invariants remain informative about its earliest history.

25:19.020 --> 25:20.580
But what about the entropy objection?

25:21.100 --> 25:24.020
The second law of thermodynamics says entropy is always increasing.

25:24.240 --> 25:25.100
Yes, it is.

25:25.100 --> 25:30.100
If the universe is fundamentally moving toward disorder, doesn't that inherently mean history

25:30.100 --> 25:31.820
is being systematically erased?

25:32.040 --> 25:36.860
It is a really common intuition, but Flyxion points out it is a major conflation of microscopic

25:36.860 --> 25:38.520
and macroscopic information.

25:38.740 --> 25:39.100
How so?

25:39.300 --> 25:44.900
Entropy growth just means the number of possible microscopic states compatible with our macroscopic

25:44.900 --> 25:46.180
observation is growing.

25:46.460 --> 25:51.860
It causes a loss of accessible distinctions at a highly specific microscopic resolution.

25:52.040 --> 25:53.300
Okay, so the tiny detail.

25:53.300 --> 25:53.900
Exactly.

25:54.200 --> 25:59.040
The exact position of every single star becomes completely randomized and unrecoverable.

25:59.360 --> 25:59.560
Right.

25:59.680 --> 26:03.860
The phase mixing and chaotic orbits make it impossible to track individual stars backward

26:03.860 --> 26:04.360
in time.

26:04.440 --> 26:04.860
Exactly.

26:05.360 --> 26:10.720
But in low-dimensional projections, like looking at the single vector of overall angular momentum

26:10.720 --> 26:14.140
correlations, can perfectly survive entropy growth.

26:14.280 --> 26:15.020
That is amazing.

26:15.360 --> 26:21.840
The microscopic details are lost to entropy, but the macroscopic structural distinction survives.

26:21.840 --> 26:28.060
So entropy randomized all the tiny variables, but it didn't erase the massive, overarching

26:28.060 --> 26:29.460
geometric relationship.

26:29.700 --> 26:30.300
Precisely.

26:30.520 --> 26:35.500
And to make this epistemic reality rigorous, Flyxion introduces a framework to help us

26:35.500 --> 26:37.200
understand what we're actually measuring.

26:38.080 --> 26:40.000
The three cones of persistence.

26:40.740 --> 26:44.320
This feels crucial for not misinterpreting astronomical data.

26:44.460 --> 26:45.640
It is absolutely essential.

26:45.800 --> 26:48.260
Imagine three nested cones, one inside the other.

26:48.380 --> 26:49.160
Okay, I'm picturing it.

26:49.160 --> 26:52.360
The outermost and largest is the physical persistence cone.

26:52.740 --> 26:58.700
This represents the set of all scales, times, and observables, where a genuine physical statistical

26:58.700 --> 27:01.760
dependence on the primordial influence still exists in the universe.

27:02.000 --> 27:02.880
Okay, so that's reality.

27:03.120 --> 27:03.420
Yes.

27:04.200 --> 27:10.200
It is the raw objective truth of reality, entirely independent of whether humans have the technology

27:10.200 --> 27:10.960
to measure it.

27:10.960 --> 27:12.840
Okay, the universe's actual memory.

27:13.020 --> 27:14.600
What is the next cone inside that?

27:14.600 --> 27:16.340
The observable persistence cone.

27:16.460 --> 27:18.000
This is a much smaller subset.

27:18.000 --> 27:23.840
It represents what our theoretically available observational channels, like the interfield

27:23.840 --> 27:27.060
spectroscopy from Menge, could potentially see in principle.

27:27.600 --> 27:27.660
Right.

27:27.760 --> 27:32.100
If the physical signal is there, but it's only expressed in a dark matter interaction that

27:32.100 --> 27:37.380
we have zero theoretical instruments to detect, it exists in the physical cone, but falls

27:37.380 --> 27:38.940
outside the observable cone.

27:39.020 --> 27:39.220
Good.

27:39.380 --> 27:41.180
And the smallest innermost cone?

27:41.340 --> 27:42.220
The detection cone.

27:42.400 --> 27:43.840
This is the narrowest reality.

27:43.840 --> 27:49.980
This is what our specific current sample sizes, our specific signal-to-noise ratios, our current

27:49.980 --> 27:55.380
reconstruction algorithms, like elizuzide, and our strict statistical thresholds, like demanding

27:55.380 --> 27:58.640
a seven-sigma proof, can actually confirm today.

27:58.640 --> 28:01.740
Okay, so to synthesize that, it's like a radio station.

28:01.880 --> 28:02.400
Let's hear it.

28:02.480 --> 28:07.120
The actual signal propagating through the air across the entire state is the physical

28:07.120 --> 28:07.860
persistence cone.

28:08.020 --> 28:08.480
It's out there.

28:08.780 --> 28:13.720
Owning a radio receiver that is technically capable of tuning into that frequency is the

28:13.720 --> 28:15.160
observable persistence cone.

28:15.280 --> 28:15.620
Yes.

28:15.900 --> 28:22.380
And actually being able to hear the song clearly through the static in your specific location

28:22.380 --> 28:25.800
with your specific volume settings is the detection cone.

28:26.040 --> 28:27.660
That is a brilliant way to put it, yes.

28:27.660 --> 28:31.660
And this nesting prevents massive scientific errors.

28:31.940 --> 28:32.420
How so?

28:32.540 --> 28:37.280
Well, for instance, in Sheng's data, they noted that this amazing memory correlation

28:37.280 --> 28:44.060
degrades and seems to vanish in lower mass halos, specifically in galaxies below 10 to the

28:44.060 --> 28:45.660
12.5 solar masses.

28:45.840 --> 28:47.080
Okay, so smaller galaxies.

28:47.260 --> 28:51.800
If I just glance at the abstract, I might assume, oh, small galaxies get knocked around too

28:51.800 --> 28:52.000
much.

28:52.060 --> 28:52.320
Yeah.

28:52.540 --> 28:53.960
Their memory is physically erased.

28:53.960 --> 28:54.440
Exactly.

28:54.860 --> 28:58.720
A careless reading collapses the quons and assumes the physical memory is just gone.

28:59.100 --> 29:02.040
But Flyxion's framework forces us to look closer.

29:02.320 --> 29:03.280
What's really going on?

29:03.440 --> 29:09.260
Sheng's team explicitly attributed this degradation to growing mathematical errors in how the Elia-Lucid

29:09.260 --> 29:13.060
algorithm remaps Lagrangian space at those smaller scales.

29:13.060 --> 29:15.660
Oh, so it's an algorithm problem, not a universe problem.

29:15.760 --> 29:19.540
Just because you hear static on your radio, it doesn't mean the DJ went home.

29:19.940 --> 29:25.220
It just means your receiver isn't sensitive enough to process that specific frequency yet.

29:25.280 --> 29:25.840
Precisely.

29:26.360 --> 29:31.660
The detection cone shrank because of algorithmic limitations in tracing smaller spectral resolutions.

29:31.660 --> 29:38.020
The physical cone, the actual memory in those smaller galaxies might still be perfectly intact.

29:38.220 --> 29:40.000
We just can't prove it yet.

29:40.180 --> 29:44.160
This framework is elegant because it tells us exactly where the boundaries of our knowledge

29:44.160 --> 29:44.540
are.

29:44.780 --> 29:48.460
But, you know, any good theoretical framework can't just explain past results.

29:48.560 --> 29:49.560
It has to predict the future.

29:49.680 --> 29:50.640
It has to be falsifiable.

29:50.820 --> 29:52.600
That's the hallmark of good science.

29:52.940 --> 29:56.980
Did Flyxion stick their neck out and make a hard prediction that could prove this whole

29:56.980 --> 29:57.760
theory wrong?

29:57.760 --> 30:03.840
They absolutely did, and they based it on a very specific empirical anomaly in Sheng's

30:03.840 --> 30:04.220
dataset.

30:04.400 --> 30:05.060
What was the anomaly?

30:05.360 --> 30:09.680
Sheng's team measured the spin using two different tracers in the same galaxies.

30:10.320 --> 30:14.140
The gas, measured via H-alpha emission, and the stars.

30:14.640 --> 30:15.920
Okay, gas and stars.

30:16.200 --> 30:20.740
And they found that the spins traced by the gas correlate with the primordial field far more

30:20.740 --> 30:22.920
strongly than the spins traced by the stars.

30:23.400 --> 30:24.060
Wait, really?

30:24.520 --> 30:27.000
Why would the gas remember better than the stars?

30:27.000 --> 30:32.740
I mean, they are sitting in the exact same galaxy, experiencing the exact same merger

30:32.740 --> 30:33.240
history.

30:33.420 --> 30:37.300
Well, Sheng attributed this discrepancy to a few mundane statistical factors.

30:37.640 --> 30:38.080
Like what?

30:38.360 --> 30:43.820
The galaxies in their sample that had easily measurable gas tended to reside in higher mass

30:43.820 --> 30:47.280
halos, which Eolucide reconstructs more accurately.

30:48.220 --> 30:53.420
Also, measuring gas kinematics inherently has smaller margins of error than measuring stellar

30:53.420 --> 30:53.960
kinematics.

30:54.200 --> 30:55.320
Okay, so observation bias.

30:55.320 --> 30:55.840
Partly.

30:56.340 --> 31:00.380
But thirdly, and most importantly, there is a physical dynamical proposition.

31:01.120 --> 31:06.180
Gas simply retains dynamical coherence better than a collisionless population of stars.

31:06.560 --> 31:08.240
I want to dig into that physical mechanism.

31:08.760 --> 31:12.260
Why does gas behave differently than stars when a galaxy gets scrambled?

31:12.460 --> 31:15.060
It comes down to collisional versus collisionless physics.

31:15.060 --> 31:16.960
Gas is a collisional fluid.

31:17.180 --> 31:18.260
Meaning the particles hit each other.

31:18.260 --> 31:18.860
Exactly.

31:19.280 --> 31:23.660
When gas clouds orbit and interact, the particles physically crash into each other.

31:24.200 --> 31:28.440
